Heel-Bow Steppers

Ingredients: CROATIAN LIJERICA + STEPPERS DUB / ROOTS-DUB PRESSURE + PREPARED ELECTRIC PIANO + FRAME DRUM + CONTRABASS CLARINET + SHORT ANALOG SUB

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during a late-summer residency inside a retired limestone dispatch warehouse above the Dubrovnik coast, where lijerica player Ivana Radić was rehearsing seated dance-accompaniment figures while Bristol dub producer Ellis Ward tested a stripped steppers rhythm through a small analog mixer. The connection appeared when Ellis noticed that Ivana’s right foot was marking pulse independently of the bow. Instead of replacing that physical beat with a programmed kick, he left the heel-stamp audible and wrote the electronic rhythm around the disagreement between bowed phrase, foot pulse, and dub delay. Prepared electric piano, frame drum, contrabass clarinet, and a tightly controlled analog sub were added to create a form where one seated performer can generate melody, drone, bow friction, and a separate percussive clock at the same time.

Heel-Bow Steppers is a dry acoustic-dub form built around the coexistence of bowed time and stamped time. Steppers dub supplies the chassis: unwavering low kick pressure, restrained snare or rim placement, mono sub, filtered chord stabs, long but disciplined delay returns, and arrangements that develop by subtraction, feedback, and mute movement rather than harmonic spectacle. The Croatian lijerica supplies a different physical grammar: a compact three-string bowed instrument held upright at the knee, capable of narrow modal melody, sustained drone relations, rough bow grain, and rapid ornamental turns.

The lijerica is recorded with one close condenser aimed toward the bow-string contact area, one ribbon microphone near the pear-shaped body, one floor boundary microphone near the player’s stamping foot, and one restrained room microphone. The close channel captures bow hair, pitch detail, finger contact, and rosin scrape. The ribbon captures wood and lower resonance. The floor microphone is treated as a musical source rather than incidental noise: heel weight, shoe sole, chair vibration, and stone-floor reflection become part of the meter. The room microphone remains low so the whole setup feels architectural but never washed out.

Players favor open-string drones, tonic-fifth cells, minor thirds, narrow seconds, short descending turns, repeated two- to five-note figures, rough unisons, brief bow tremolo, and dry stopped notes. Long lyrical fiddle solos are minimized. The style depends less on melodic quantity than on the relationship between bow phrase length and the independently maintained foot pulse.

The central compositional device is the Heel Ledger. Every section assigns three simultaneous timing roles: BOW, HEEL, and DUB. BOW defines the length and accent shape of the lijerica phrase. HEEL defines the seated player’s physical pulse, usually in quarters, dotted quarters, or alternating heavy/light stamps. DUB defines the machine grid and echo return. These layers share tempo but are not required to align on every barline.

A four-beat steppers kick may continue beneath a five-accent bowed figure while the heel marks only beats one and three. In another section, the bow may sustain across two bars while the heel continues every quarter note and an unsynchronized tape return lands between them. Development comes from changing which of the three timing systems feels dominant.

The heel-stamp is never turned into a gimmick trigger. No sensor is required and no kick must fire from the foot. The physical sound is recorded acoustically and mixed as its own transient layer. Some stamps reinforce the electronic kick; others occur alone; others are muted from the mix while the player continues stamping so the ensemble can still feel the underlying body pulse.

Steppers drums remain heavy but sparse. The kick is round, short, and centered. Snare and rim are dry, with occasional spring reverb sends that are muted quickly. Closed hats are minimal. Percussion density is intentionally lower than standard dub so bow attack and foot impact remain legible.

Sub bass is mono and controlled. It favors roots, fifths, octave drops, and brief chromatic approach notes. Long reese textures, wobble bass, and trap-style slides are excluded. The bass line often enters after a heel-stamp rather than with it, creating a physical call-and-weight relationship without direct triggering.

Prepared electric piano supplies compact harmonic punctuation. Felt strips, paper damping, and hand-muted tines create short fifths, suspended seconds, minor sevenths, and dry octave shapes. The piano rarely sustains beneath the lijerica. Its most important role is to mark places where the BOW and HEEL layers temporarily coincide, then disappear again.

Frame drum provides a second human percussion color. Finger taps, muted center hits, rim knocks, and short rolls are used as replacements for selected electronic percussion events. The player avoids continuous accompaniment. When the frame drum enters, one programmed layer usually leaves.

Contrabass clarinet supplies low acoustic air. It plays chalumeau fundamentals, breath-led attacks, short descending fourths, key noise, and occasional split-tone edges. It appears mainly when the lijerica moves upward, filling the low-mid space without competing with the mono sub.

Dub effects are treated as architecture. One tape or modeled tape delay carries selected electric-piano stabs, rim hits, and occasional lijerica phrase endings. Delay time may sit slightly away from exact note values so returns slowly migrate against the heel pulse. Spring reverb is short enough to retain transient identity. Feedback is automated by hand over long spans rather than used for dramatic one-bar throws.

The defining tension is seated bodily pulse versus studio pulse. The machine can be perfectly stable, but the music never feels disembodied because a shoe keeps striking real stone underneath it.

Genre DNA

* Tempo & Pulse: 116–124 BPM, usually 4/4 steppers pressure with quarter-note kick, sparse rim or snare, minimal hats, audible heel-stamps, and lijerica phrases whose accent counts may extend across barlines.
* Harmonic Palette: Dorian and Aeolian centers, South Adriatic modal inflections used non-dogmatically, open fifths, minor thirds, suspended 2nds, minor 7ths, octave pedals, drone tones, and occasional semitone friction.
* Production Signatures:
    1. Heel Ledger — BOW, HEEL, and DUB function as three independent but tempo-related timing systems.
    2. Stone-Floor Pulse — the lijerica player’s real heel-stamp is close-recorded and preserved as a musical transient rather than cleaned away.
    3. Weight-After-Step — selected sub notes enter just after physical stamps, creating delayed low-frequency weight without trigger mapping.
    4. Coincidence Piano — prepared electric-piano dyads appear mainly where bow and heel accents temporarily align.
    5. Vacancy Drum Rule — frame-drum attacks replace machine percussion rather than stacking on top of it.
    6. Migrating Dub Return — delay time is allowed to sit slightly off-grid so repeats drift around the heel pulse while the dry performance stays stable.
* Dynamics: Intensity grows through stronger foot impact, denser bow articulation, wider sub-register drops, brighter electric-piano attacks, higher delay feedback, and more forceful contrabass-clarinet entries. Peaks remain spacious, grounded, and pressure-led rather than explosive.

Scene & Origin Lore

Heel-Bow Steppers formed inside a decommissioned limestone sorting and coastal shipping warehouse outside Dubrovnik. The building retained pale stone dust, timber pallet racks, rusted weighing scales, steel hand trucks, faded blue dispatch numbers, rope slings, cargo hooks, canvas sacks, narrow clerestory windows, and a heavy flagstone floor that made every seated heel-stamp unusually clear.

Lijerica player Ivana Radić had been using the warehouse to document old regional dance-accompaniment techniques. She played seated with the instrument supported at the knee and kept time physically with her right foot. The warehouse floor exaggerated that pulse enough that it sounded almost like a second percussionist.

Bristol producer Ellis Ward was working in a neighboring room on stripped steppers dub. His first attempt removed the foot noise and quantized Ivana’s bowing against the kick. The result sounded polished but anonymous. He restored the floor microphone, muted half the hats, and let one five-accent bowed figure continue over the four-beat machine pattern while Ivana stamped steady quarters. The three clocks immediately became the track’s identity.

Prepared-electric-piano player Petra Marin joined after discovering that short damped chords could mark rare alignment points without filling the room. Frame-drum player Luka Benić added sparse acoustic attacks only where programmed percussion had been removed. Contrabass clarinetist Maeve Holloway completed the ensemble with low-air tones that connected the lijerica body resonance to the dub sub.

Their first performances were called dispatch readings. A narrow board beside the stage showed three handwritten rows labeled BOW, HEEL, and DUB. No electronics were driven from the chart. The diagram simply made clear that three simultaneous timing systems were allowed to disagree.

The visual language developed around limestone white, faded shipping blue, rust brown, dark rope fiber, black wool, dull brass, chalk grey, and low amber work light. The unofficial rule is that if the heel can no longer be heard or felt, the arrangement has lost its body.

